# Commit & PR title convention

Every commit message and every PR title **must** follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<optional-scope>): <description>
```

## Format rules

- **type** (required): one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`
- **scope** (optional): short area — `api`, `web`, `ui`, `api-client`, `infra`, `agent`, `auth`
- **description**: imperative, lowercase start, no trailing period, ≤72 chars preferred
- Body (optional): blank line after subject, then why/details
- Breaking change: `feat(api)!: ...` or `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the footer

## Examples

```
feat(api): add collections CRUD with ownership checks
fix(web): gate API queries until auth token getter is ready
docs: complete README and submission writeups
chore: scaffold pnpm monorepo and ESLint
refactor(api): decouple collection access from Prisma repository
```

## Forbidden

- Sentence-case subjects without a type (`Add X`, `Fix Y`, `Update Z`)
- WIP / temp / “asdf” messages
- Squashing history that erases meaningful conventional commits before submit (unless explicitly requested)

## PR titles

PR titles use the same convention as commit subjects, e.g.:

```
feat: private bookmark manager with Auth0, privacy tests, and web UI
```

Prefer a single overarching type that matches the PR’s primary purpose (`feat` for new product capability, `fix` for bugfix PRs, etc.).
